        "document_type": "Central Bank Monetary Policy Decision",
        "summary": "The Monetary Policy Board decided to increase the one-week repo auction interest rate from 42.5% to 46%, the overnight lending rate from 46% to 49%, and the overnight borrowing rate from 41% to 44.5%. The decision was made on April 17, 2025, under decision number 2025-24. The Board noted that underlying inflation trends declined in March, with core inflation expected to rise slightly in April due to financial market developments, while services inflation is expected to move sideways. Underlying inflation expectations and pricing behavior remain a risk to the disinflation process. The firm monetary policy stance supports disinflation through domestic demand stabilization, real appreciation of the Turkish lira, and improved inflation expectations, with fiscal policy coordination providing additional support. A tight monetary policy stance will be maintained until a sustained decline in inflation and price stability are achieved. Policy rates will be set based on inflation outcomes, trends, and expectations to deliver the required tightness in the disinflation process. The Board will determine policy rate steps using an inflation outlook-focused, cautious, and meeting-by-meeting approach, and will tighten the policy stance if a significant and persistent deterioration in inflation is anticipated. Recent developments in financial markets have led to additional steps to support the monetary transmission mechanism, with liquidity conditions closely monitored and liquidity management tools used effectively. The Board will set policy rates to ensure monetary and financial conditions that will reduce underlying inflation and bring inflation to the 5% medium-term target, using all monetary policy tools resolutely. Decisions will be made in a predictable, data-driven, and transparent framework. The Monetary Policy Board Meeting Summary will be published within five business days.",
